cartridge "GOING UP?/GAZETTE/1987"

' GOING UP?
' A ColecoVision game based on "Going Up?", COMPUTE!'s Gazette disk, June
' 1987 (Commodore 64 machine language, SYS 2063). Made for Amy Studio by
' Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: a worker has to cross the four floors of a tower that is all
' lift shafts. Seven lift cars run up and down their shafts without
' stopping, each at its own speed, and wrap round from the roof to the
' basement. Walk floor 1 from right to left, ride the landing's platform up,
' walk floor 2 left to right, and so on; after floor 4 the platform takes
' you off the roof. A car that touches the worker in a shaft knocks him
' flat. Each floor is worth 100, the bonus clock (1000) what is left of it
' at the end of the level. The cars get faster every level.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick walks left and right. On the
' title, up and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' From the original (orig/goingup.dis, run in jsc64): seven cars, the odd
' ones going down, the even ones up, each moving one pixel every D passes
' of the main loop with D = 40, 60, 55, 50, 45, 65, 70 ($10B2); every new
' level takes 4 off each D, never under 8 ($11E2), and puts all the cars
' back in the hidden band ($11CB); the worker moves a pixel every 45 loop
' passes going left and every 48 going right ($0FB0/$0FC8); floors 1 and
' 3 are walked right to left, 2 and 4 left to right ($0E96); the landings
' are safe (the hit test only counts between X $22 and $FC, $0E37); 100 a
' floor ($0ED6); the bonus starts at 1000 and loses 1 every 230 passes
' ($1111), is added to the score after floor 4 ($11A8) and is set back to
' 1000 after a death; a bonus run down to 0 ends the game at once ($114D);
' five men, one more at levels 5, 10 and 15 ($1190); after a death the man
' starts the same floor again from its start ($0E69).
'
' Liberties: the C64's main loop has no fixed rate: its passes, measured
' against the jiffy clock while walking (about 39 a frame), set the speeds
' here, scaled by 0.8 for the smaller tower (cars 7987 / D 256ths of a pixel
' a frame, 0.78 for the fastest at level 1; the man 0.74 left, 0.70 right);
' the tower is 7 shafts 20 pixels apart with cars 10 wide (the C64's 29 and
' 15) and floors 40 apart (48), so the score panel fits on the right; hits
' are box tests on the man's body (4 pixels wide) and the car (the C64 used
' sprite collisions, its arms and legs could touch too); the bonus loses 1
' every 6 frames; the bonus is counted into the score with a tick (the C64
' added it at once); no pause key (the C64 paused on SHIFT); a sound for
' every step, floor and level; GAME OVER then the title (the C64 waited for
' the button and started again).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

